{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790966805,"nonce":"SAWSCwDcZ50gjQix6eASxrMX1zE=","title":"Toast exit motion doesn't play on web (wasm); enter motion works, exit snaps","message":"On web (wasm), `cn::toast` exit animation doesn't play — toasts snap out of the tray instead of sliding away. Enter animation works fine. Desktop runs both enter and exit correctly.\n\nToasts use `motion_derived(motion_key).enter_animation(...).exit_animation(...)` via the `OverlayBuilder` route, NOT the CSS `@keyframes` path that other cn surfaces use. So this isn't related to the class-only-anim-start fix (commit 692243a1).\n\nBoth web.rs Phase 1b and Phase 4 call `process_global_motion_exit_starts()`, and Phase 1c calls `toast_tray().lock().unwrap().update(now)` which drives the dismiss → `queue_global_motion_exit_start` chain. The pieces are in place but something between \"exit queued\" and \"motion FSM in Exiting state advances per frame\" isn't landing on the web runner.\n\nLikely candidates:\n- Frame ordering: `process_global_motion_exit_starts` in Phase 1b runs BEFORE `toast_tray.update(now)` in Phase 1c, so on the frame the toast auto-dismisses, the queued exit doesn't get drained until next frame. Desktop has the same ordering (windowed.rs:4917, 4938) and works there — so probably not the root cause.\n- The toast tray's `take_dirty()` rebuild happens immediately after dismiss, possibly removing the toast from the rendered subtree before the motion FSM has a chance to attach an active motion.\n- `motion_derived` lookup might not survive the toast tray subtree rebuild on web (stable_id race like the class-anim-first-interaction bug, but for motion_key resolution).\n\n**Why:** Web's Phase 1c subtree rebuild path was added new (commit 6fa965dc) and might not preserve motion state across the rebuild the same way desktop's does.\n\n**How to apply:** If you're touching `web.rs` Phase 1c or `motion_derived`, this is the open issue. Test by pushing a toast and waiting for auto-dismiss — on web the toast disappears instantly; on desktop it slides out.","files":null}]}